  "limitations": [
    "Current scanning technologies confine multisensory, three-dimensional experience of the built environment to a motionless condition within a lab, limiting their ability to capture the full architectural experience. (page 28)",
    "Architects have been reluctant to participate in discussions integrating new scientific and humanistic models due to intensifying technological and professional demands pushing humanities out of curricula. (page 28)",
    "Architectural design studios remain uncritically saddled with conventional axioms regarding the objectification of form ('building as object'). (page 28)"
  ],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This book, \"Architecture and Embodiment,\" explores the implications of new scientific and humanistic models for architectural design, moving beyond traditional dualistic views of mind and body. It argues that built environments profoundly restructure human cognitive abilities and outlooks, and that our embodied natures, emotions, and social interactions are deeply intertwined with how we perceive and engage with architecture. Key findings include the brain's plasticity in response to environmental stimuli, the precognitive role of emotions in evaluating environments, and the function of mirror neurons in embodied simulation and animating inanimate spaces. The author advocates for shifting design focus from objects to the human experience within built environments. The introduction notes limitations in current neuroimaging technologies for capturing multisensory architectural experience and critiques the architectural profession's reluctance to integrate these scientific insights.",
